WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.420 --> 00:00:07.420
When historians look back at
this time 500 years from now,

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:09.910
they will put a breaking point right

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:11.510
where we build a space elevator.

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:17.370
This is pre-space and this was post.

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:20.970
This is an entire new society.

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:22.053
Everything changes.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:32.000
Imagine simply climbing
a cable into space.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.783
No rocket, just press the
up button and you're there.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:41.770
The concept is known
as the space elevator.

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:43.910
Not only could it significantly change how

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:46.220
we leave planet Earth, but it could

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:47.930
also completely transform

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:50.463
humanity's relationship with space.

00:00:51.378 --> 00:00:53.260
And there are scientists who think

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:54.810
we could have already built it.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.350
We have the material,
we have the technology,

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:01.400
we have the lasers, we have the
climbers, we have all of it.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:04.120
If a private entity wants to build it

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:06.830
or a government, they could build it.

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:08.330
It'll change society.

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:12.390
We have no clue how colonizing into space,

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:13.803
how really spreading out into space

00:01:13.803 --> 00:01:15.700
will really change our society

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:19.200
over the next 50 to 200 years.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.067
It'll be completely different.

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:36.460
Ideas of a space
elevator-type device extend

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:38.773
as far back as the 19th century.

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:42.460
Russian and American scientists came up

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:44.620
with various theories
for a structure built

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:47.543
from Earth's surface that
would reach the stars.

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:52.730
By 1979, the concept was
relayed to a larger audience

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:55.367
by writer Arthur C Clark in his novel

00:01:55.367 --> 00:01:57.247
"The Fountains of Paradise."

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:01.250
I am happy that people are taking it more

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:03.720
and more seriously, and I do think

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:05.923
that it may be the way to space.

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.760
But these ideas struggle to advance

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:11.180
beyond the realms of science fiction

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:14.100
as we continue to rely on
tried and tested methods

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.453
of getting into space.

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:20.810
Right now, we've got rockets.
They use a lot of fuel.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:23.720
They take a lot of fuel
to get up into space.

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:26.500
They have to carry fuel to lift the fuel

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.270
to lift them into space.

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:32.100
If you want to go to
geo-synchronous orbit, it's millions

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:33.610
of dollars per pound if you want to go

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:35.680
to the moon or Mars.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:36.900
It's expensive.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:40.210
With the space elevator, we
reduce the cost of getting

00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:43.920
into space to dollars per pound.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.770
Each climber will ascend
the ribbon every day,

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:50.863
every couple days, and it'll be taking 15,

00:02:50.863 --> 00:02:54.960
23, 100 tons into orbit,
which is comparable

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:58.173
to what we launch now
over the course of a year.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:04.230
So how does the space elevator even work?

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:07.110
The basic idea is a cable
would be pulled taut

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:08.990
thanks to a counterweight.

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:11.750
It stays vertical because
the counterweight is thrown

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:14.090
outward by the Earth's rotation

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:16.343
and the base is held down by gravity.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:21.370
Take a look at this ball
on the end of this string.

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:24.680
If you swing the ball in a
circle at a sufficient speed,

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:28.123
the rope will become taut,
revolving around your hand.

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:33.430
The force pulling the rope taut
is centrifugal acceleration.

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:36.070
This force generated by
the rotation of the Earth

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:39.480
in space will pull the space
elevator tether upwards

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:41.983
into space and away from the Earth.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:48.850
In reality, the ball would have
to be a large counterweight

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:51.710
and positioned above
geo-synchronous orbit,

00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:56.710
that's 22,236 miles above
the Earth's surface.

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:58.800
This is where the outward force pulling it

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:02.580
away from Earth is greater than
the downward pull of gravity

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:05.123
and that keeps the elevator line taut.

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:09.120
Early ideas suggested
building a cable from Earth

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:11.720
up into space, but the materials needed

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:14.963
to withstand such enormous
weight don't exist.

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:21.730
Physicist Bradley Edwards
breathed new life into the theory.

00:04:21.730 --> 00:04:24.040
I've been working on the space elevator

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:27.150
along with a number of other space systems

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:30.340
and new technologies for the better part

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:32.440
of 30-some years.

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:34.670
I searched for years to find a reason

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:37.370
why we couldn't build a space elevator.

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:40.030
The materials, the
climbers, the power beaming,

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:43.920
the low-Earth orbit debris,

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:47.550
radiation, just any number of things

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:49.670
why couldn't we build it.

00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:52.750
There was no reason and
that was where it started

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:57.750
and I wrote a paper up in 1998
with everything I had found,

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.130
how you would design it,
how you would build it,

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:03.140
any problems, issues that I found.

00:05:03.140 --> 00:05:05.577
And I published it thinking, "Well, okay,

00:05:05.577 --> 00:05:08.057
"someone's gonna published
the next paper saying,

00:05:08.057 --> 00:05:11.297
"'Okay, great, Brad, but you know what?

00:05:11.297 --> 00:05:12.587
"'You forgot this, this, this, and this,

00:05:12.587 --> 00:05:14.240
"'and so we can't build it.'"

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:17.403
It never happened.

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:21.770
Brad Edwards' proposal was written

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:25.573
for NIAC, NASA's Institute
for Advanced Concepts.

00:05:26.643 --> 00:05:28.360
NIAC wanted to look at those things

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:30.790
that might really change the future.

00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:34.420
So we put out a broad
call for innovative ideas.

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:37.750
We got an interesting
batch of proposals right

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:40.440
off the bat, and one of those came

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:42.990
from a researcher, Brad Edwards.

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:46.080
One of NASA initial
responses when we brought it

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:48.500
to the table as a funded study,

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:51.000
before we ever brought it up, they sort

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:53.537
of dismissively said, "We hope
you're not bringing anything

00:05:53.537 --> 00:05:57.077
"like a space elevator in as
part of your funded studies."

00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:01.257
To NASA's credit, they said,
"Okay, we understand now

00:06:01.257 --> 00:06:03.230
"why you're gonna pursue this,"

00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:05.770
and, you know, "proceed."

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:09.360
The problem with earlier versions
of the space elevator was

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:14.360
we just didn't have a
way to build the cable

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:18.640
that was needed to
maintain a space elevator.

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:21.560
And Brad came up with an
innovative twist looking

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:24.610
into the future on where
carbon nanotubes might go

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:26.670
in terms of strength.

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:30.180
Carbon nanotubes are
something of a super material.

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:33.150
Despite their tiny size,
they're theoretically stronger

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:35.313
than things like Kevlar or graphene.

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:38.710
Brad Edwards suggested launching a rocket

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:41.163
which would carry the
carbon nanotube cable.

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:43.820
One end of the cable would then be dropped

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:44.963
back towards Earth.

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:48.520
The cable will be light
enough and strong enough

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:51.650
to build just a thin ribbon
which robots could climb

00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:55.260
to build more ribbon, eventually
becoming strong enough

00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:58.330
to carry heavy payloads
such as solar panels,

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:00.873
satellites, and even tourists.

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:05.390
So you have to launch all
this mass to begin with

00:07:05.390 --> 00:07:08.513
and then start building the elevator.

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:12.120
Hence, the earlier version that had

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:16.920
incredibly massive cables
just were impractical.

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:20.410
But now, if the projection
of the links were

00:07:20.410 --> 00:07:25.110
to work out, then maybe
you'd have something

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:27.010
that could become feasible.

00:07:27.010 --> 00:07:29.980
A few years ago, the
only carbon nanofibers

00:07:29.980 --> 00:07:31.814
were microscopic.

00:07:31.814 --> 00:07:33.490
A couple years later,
they were millimeters.

00:07:33.490 --> 00:07:35.870
Now we're talking about
things that are centimeters.

00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:38.530
And if you project that growth

00:07:38.530 --> 00:07:42.280
because people are putting
more and more interest

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:44.790
in carbon nanofibers, then in 20 years,

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:48.303
we might have carbon nanofibers
that are kilometers long.

00:07:50.230 --> 00:07:52.160
While there've been
huge steps in advancing

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:55.580
the technology, some people
still have their doubts.

00:07:55.580 --> 00:07:57.920
Carbon nanotubes exist, they're available.

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:02.630
But there hasn't been money
going into commercializing them.

00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:06.570
We've made them, we have
put them into plastics,

00:08:06.570 --> 00:08:09.610
and increase it dramatically.

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:11.320
The strongest steel has the strength

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:13.640
of about five gigapascals.

00:08:13.640 --> 00:08:18.640
Carbon fibers, Kevlar, these
conventional materials,

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:22.400
they will have strengths
of about the same,

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:26.070
anywhere between two
and seven gigapascals.

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:29.370
Carbon nanotubes, these are measured

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:33.200
at 150 to 200 gigapascals.

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:37.960
What we need to build a
space elevator is about 60.

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:42.300
So carbon nanotubes in
these bags are three times

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:44.803
as strong as we need to
make the space elevator.

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:48.720
And we can grow them longer.

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:50.870
So what about the cost
of carbon nanotubes?

00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:52.410
Is this the biggest hurdle?

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:55.200
Literally in our system,
it costs us about $1 a gram

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:57.233
right now, on a small scale.

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:03.260
We turn that into production,
the costs are gonna go down.

00:09:04.650 --> 00:09:09.650
Now, at $1 a gram, that's
pretty expensive and we need

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:12.900
about 1,000 tons to build
the first space elevator.

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:15.520
That's going to be a billion dollars.

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:17.760
A billion dollars is a lot of money.

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:20.890
But when you talk about
building a space elevator

00:09:20.890 --> 00:09:23.470
for a total of $8 billion, a large chunk

00:09:23.470 --> 00:09:25.370
of it being those carbon nanotubes,

00:09:25.370 --> 00:09:28.290
what you can launch with that system,

00:09:28.290 --> 00:09:31.960
we easily spend that money
launching satellites.

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:34.250
And whatever else we might launch

00:09:34.250 --> 00:09:37.763
over the next 10, 20 years,
it's not that expensive.

00:09:39.550 --> 00:09:42.590
To put that $8 billion figure in context,

00:09:42.590 --> 00:09:44.270
NASA just spent 10 billion

00:09:44.270 --> 00:09:47.330
on the recently launched
James Webb telescope.

00:09:47.330 --> 00:09:50.160
And if Brad Edwards' cost
estimate is accurate,

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:52.440
the world's space-faring
nations spent enough

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:55.783
to cover 10 space elevators in 2020 alone.

00:09:56.680 --> 00:09:59.810
NASA chose not to pursue
the space elevator concept

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:01.630
despite things like carbon nanotubes

00:10:01.630 --> 00:10:04.160
getting longer and stronger.

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:05.610
So why do you think that was?

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:08.930
A space elevator doesn't
exist in isolation.

00:10:08.930 --> 00:10:11.040
It exists along with everything else

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:13.770
that's in orbit in Earth.

00:10:13.770 --> 00:10:17.520
And while Brad came up
with a very clever design

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:20.450
to allow his space elevator tether

00:10:20.450 --> 00:10:24.430
to survive a few hits every now and then,

00:10:24.430 --> 00:10:26.060
and he came up with some approaches

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:29.260
to allow the space elevator
to do an elaborate dance

00:10:29.260 --> 00:10:32.670
to avoid larger satellites
that might be coming

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:37.670
through its path, the space
debris issue today is a real,

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:40.843
real challenge for a
space elevator concept.

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:47.410
There's an estimated 128 million objects

00:10:47.410 --> 00:10:50.380
smaller than a centimeter
orbiting the Earth

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:53.240
along with another
900,000 that measure one

00:10:53.240 --> 00:10:56.320
to 10 centimeters, all with the potential

00:10:56.320 --> 00:10:58.313
to cause significant damage.

00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:02.500
Brad Edwards' concept would
see a space elevator mounted

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:04.600
to a mobile oil rig-like structure

00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:06.623
to dodge any dangerous material.

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:10.720
It's really hard to
imagine that you could have

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:13.850
a space elevator that
could survive the number

00:11:13.850 --> 00:11:16.870
of collisions that one would expect

00:11:16.870 --> 00:11:18.980
over the lifetime of a space elevator.

00:11:18.980 --> 00:11:22.340
And if the ribbon's cut,
you're having a bad day.

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:24.030
Brad, what do you think about the risks

00:11:24.030 --> 00:11:26.470
of something hitting the elevator?

00:11:26.470 --> 00:11:28.900
Tracking orbital debris,
there's commercial ventures

00:11:28.900 --> 00:11:31.900
out there that do that,
so to a large extent,

00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:33.730
we don't even have to deal with it.

00:11:33.730 --> 00:11:36.650
There's also risks on Earth, you know,

00:11:36.650 --> 00:11:40.110
a plane or a ship, or even a storm

00:11:40.110 --> 00:11:41.770
could impact the structure.

00:11:41.770 --> 00:11:46.370
If the elevator does break,
that'll be pretty devastating.

00:11:46.370 --> 00:11:49.700
You'll see the elevator in
various science fiction movies,

00:11:49.700 --> 00:11:52.870
things like that, and invariably,
the space elevator comes

00:11:52.870 --> 00:11:56.100
down and does this crashing, terrible wrap

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:58.880
around the planet, destroying
everything in its wake.

00:11:58.880 --> 00:12:01.770
Again, we're talking about
ribbon three-feet wide,

00:12:01.770 --> 00:12:03.590
thin as paper.

00:12:03.590 --> 00:12:08.020
If you break it, if you
break the top part off,

00:12:08.020 --> 00:12:11.870
the counterweight, what
happens is the ribbon starts

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:14.620
to come down and then it breaks.

00:12:14.620 --> 00:12:17.683
Because the tension increases,
it breaks a the bottom

00:12:17.683 --> 00:12:20.370
and the entire ribbon flies out

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:23.170
into space, never to be seen again.

00:12:23.170 --> 00:12:26.790
So what you get are a few
pieces of this black material,

00:12:26.790 --> 00:12:29.970
lightweight material that falls down.

00:12:29.970 --> 00:12:32.050
But it's not going to destroy a building,

00:12:32.050 --> 00:12:33.490
it's not going to break your neck.

00:12:33.490 --> 00:12:37.020
If you cut it at the bottom,
the entire space elevator files

00:12:37.020 --> 00:12:38.920
off into space never to be seen again.

00:12:41.950 --> 00:12:44.470
Another reason the elevator
hasn't materialized is

00:12:44.470 --> 00:12:46.700
the dramatic fall in price of launching

00:12:46.700 --> 00:12:49.600
into space thanks to the likes of SpaceX

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:51.253
and their reusable rockets.

00:12:52.100 --> 00:12:54.600
But as more countries and
companies are sending payloads

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:57.583
into space, a real space
economy is growing.

00:12:58.490 --> 00:13:01.210
It's estimated the global
space industry could surge

00:13:01.210 --> 00:13:04.073
to over $1 trillion by 2040.

00:13:05.380 --> 00:13:07.870
A space elevator could bring huge returns

00:13:07.870 --> 00:13:10.730
for the team that builds
it, which is maybe why

00:13:10.730 --> 00:13:14.173
a Japanese construction company
is funding some research.

00:13:15.620 --> 00:13:18.020
A team from Nihon University in Tokyo are

00:13:18.020 --> 00:13:21.590
building robot climbers
that ascend synthetic cable,

00:13:21.590 --> 00:13:23.690
focusing on increasing speed

00:13:23.690 --> 00:13:25.943
and minimizing power consumption.

00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:57.320
The company backing the
research, Obayashi Corporation,

00:13:57.320 --> 00:14:00.090
believes a space elevator
could aid missions

00:14:00.090 --> 00:14:02.120
to the moon and Mars.

00:14:02.120 --> 00:14:04.470
The scientists say the biggest hurdles are

00:14:04.470 --> 00:14:05.820
beyond their control.

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:25.130
The space elevator has to be operated

00:15:25.130 --> 00:15:27.083
in a way that it benefits everyone.

00:15:27.920 --> 00:15:32.010
It has to be that most
anybody can launch on it.

00:15:32.010 --> 00:15:35.920
Nobody launches any weapons,
any military anything on it.

00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:40.313
It sort of has to go that way,
otherwise it gets destroyed.

00:15:42.260 --> 00:15:45.640
So it comes out where it's almost forced

00:15:45.640 --> 00:15:49.313
to be a positive thing,
otherwise it goes away.

00:15:52.770 --> 00:15:55.100
There's plenty holding
back a space elevator

00:15:55.100 --> 00:15:55.933
here, on Earth.

00:15:57.010 --> 00:15:59.950
Some think building one on
the moon might be the best way

00:15:59.950 --> 00:16:01.433
to prove the technology.

00:16:02.570 --> 00:16:04.950
Thanks to the moon's lack
of atmosphere, it would

00:16:04.950 --> 00:16:06.610
also mean the cable could be made

00:16:06.610 --> 00:16:10.673
from weaker existing materials,
and it'd be much cheaper.

00:16:11.710 --> 00:16:14.020
But while still useful,
it wouldn't give us

00:16:14.020 --> 00:16:18.680
the huge savings of sending
payloads into space from Earth.

00:16:18.680 --> 00:16:20.850
Either way, the space elevator is

00:16:20.850 --> 00:16:22.793
still a long, long way off.

00:16:23.960 --> 00:16:26.530
I would love to be proven wrong.

00:16:26.530 --> 00:16:28.970
If we had a space elevator,
it would be awesome.

00:16:28.970 --> 00:16:32.678
It would be paradigm-changing
because it we can get

00:16:32.678 --> 00:16:34.290
to the point of a space elevator, yes,

00:16:34.290 --> 00:16:38.170
it provides incredible access to space.

00:16:38.170 --> 00:16:39.883
So I'd love to be proven wrong.

00:16:41.290 --> 00:16:43.100
Where the space elevator might take

00:16:43.100 --> 00:16:45.520
the human species is one thing;

00:16:45.520 --> 00:16:47.900
what we bring back to Earth might be

00:16:47.900 --> 00:16:50.360
more important than we think.

00:16:50.360 --> 00:16:52.550
We can colonize the moon and Mars.

00:16:52.550 --> 00:16:55.160
We can go to the asteroids cheaply.

00:16:55.160 --> 00:16:59.750
And we can mine them for minerals, metals

00:16:59.750 --> 00:17:02.320
that we're running out
of down here, on Earth.

00:17:02.320 --> 00:17:07.010
And instead of digging up,
tearing up a pristine rainforest

00:17:07.010 --> 00:17:09.610
some place, to get to those metals

00:17:09.610 --> 00:17:14.140
underneath, we can go
to a barren rock that is

00:17:14.140 --> 00:17:17.150
nothing more than a large piece

00:17:17.150 --> 00:17:20.290
of those metals, collect
them up, bring them back

00:17:20.290 --> 00:17:23.110
to Earth without the destruction.

00:17:23.110 --> 00:17:28.110
It's changed from back in
1998, it was science fiction.

00:17:29.490 --> 00:17:32.460
You know, capturing
asteroids, bringing them

00:17:32.460 --> 00:17:34.730
in, building large cables.

00:17:34.730 --> 00:17:38.390
And now we have it and we probably

00:17:38.390 --> 00:17:40.590
could have started
building it 10 years ago.

